    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-l/q1",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q1-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The table below shows the maximum temperature T C ° on five different days and the corresponding ice cream sales, N , of a certain shop on those days. T 15 20 25 30 35 N 69 165 172 200 232 a) State, with a reason, which is the explanatory variable in the above described scenario and state the statistical name of the other variable. ( ) 2 b) Use a statistical calculator to determine … i. … the value of the product moment correlation coefficient between T and N . ( ) 1\" style=\"--madas-width:570.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q1-question-2.svg\" alt=\"ii. … the equation of the regression line between N and T , giving the answer in the form N = a + bT , where a and b are constants. ( ) 2 c) Interpret in the context of this question the physical meaning of a and b . ( ) 2 d) Use the equation of the regression line to estimate the value of N when … i. … T = 18 C ° . ii. … T = 37 C ° . iii. … T = 45 C °\" style=\"--madas-width:569.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q1-question-3.svg\" alt=\"Comment further on the reliability of each of these estimates. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:589.5px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q1-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Temperature is the explanatory variable because it is used to predict ice cream sales: [M1] The number of ice creams sold is the response variable: [A1] (b) Using a statistical calculator, (i) the correlation is r = 0.933805 . . . . [A1] (ii) The regression line is bN = −12.9 + 7.22T . [M1] [A1] (c) The intercept is the model’s predicted sales at 0◦C. This is well outside the observed temperature range, and the negative value has no practical interpretation as a count: [B1] The slope predicts an average increase of 7.22 ice creams sold per additional degree Celsius: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q1-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(d) Substitution in the rounded line gives −12.9 + 7.22(18) = 117.06, −12.9 + 7.22(37) = 254.24, −12.9 + 7.22(45) = 312.0. The estimates are 117 at 18◦C, 254 at 37◦C, and 312 at 45◦C, to the nearest ice cream: [B1] [A1] At 18◦C the estimate interpolates within the sample and the correlation is strong: [M1] [A1] At 37◦C it extrapolates slightly and is less reliable: [A1] At 45◦C it extrapolates far beyond the observations and is unreliable: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 1 is 13 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The table below shows the maximum temperature T C ° on five different days and the corresponding ice cream sales, N , of a certain shop on those days. T 15 20 25 30 35 N 69 165 172 200 232 a) State, with a reason, which is the explanatory variable in the above described scenario and state the statistical name of the other variable. ( ) 2 b) Use a statistical calculator to determine … i. … the value of the product moment correlation coefficient between T and N . ( ) 1 ii. … the equation of the regression line between N and T , giving the answer in the form N = a + bT , where a and b are constants. ( ) 2 c) Interpret in the context of this question the physical meaning of a and b . ( ) 2 d) Use the equation of the regression line to estimate the value of N when … i. … T = 18 C ° . ii. … T = 37 C ° . iii. … T = 45 C ° Comment further on the reliability of each of these estimates. ( ) 6"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-l/q2",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q2-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The probability distribution of a discrete random variable X is given by  1  x = 1, 2, 3, ... , 12 12 P ( X = x ) =   0 otherwise Determine  ( P X + 2 &lt; 3 X − 4 ≤ 2 X + 7 ) . ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:592.1px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q2-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The first inequality is equivalent to 2X &gt; 6, [B1] [A1] while the second gives X ≤11. [M1] [A1] Divide the first inequality by 2 to obtain X &gt; 3. Intersect this with X ≤11. Each admissible outcome has probability 1/12. Exactly the eight equally likely values 4, 5, . . . , 11 satisfy both, so the probability is 8/12 = 2/3 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 2 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The probability distribution of a discrete random variable X is given by  1  x = 1, 2, 3, ... , 12 12 P ( X = x ) =   0 otherwise Determine  ( P X + 2 < 3 X − 4 ≤ 2 X + 7 ) . ( ) 6"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-l/q3",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Give two advantages and two disadvantages of the method of quota sampling. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:590.8px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Quota sampling can be quick and inexpensive: [B1] The interviewer can stop recruiting from a group as soon as its required number is reached, without first constructing a complete sampling frame: The quotas can also ensure that specified groups are represented: Selection within a quota is non-random and may introduce interviewer bias: [A1] Since selection probabilities are unknown, ordinary probability-based estimates of sampling error cannot be calculated . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:674.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 3 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "Give two advantages and two disadvantages of the method of quota sampling. ( ) 3"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-l/q4",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q4-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The number of hours worked in a given week by a group of 64 freelance electricians is summarized in the table below. Hours Frequency (nearest hour) 1 – 10 5 11 – 20 16 21 – 25 14 26 – 30 17 31 – 40 10 41 – 59 2 a) Draw an accurate histogram to represent this data. ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q4-question-2.svg\" alt=\"b) Use the histogram to estimate the number of freelance electricians that worked between 15 and 37 hours during that week, correct to the nearest hour. ( ) 3 c) Estimate the median of the data. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:589.5px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q4-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Looking at the diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:606.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) The boundaries are 0.5, 10.5, 20.5, 25.5, 30.5, 40.5, 59.5. [B1] [M1] [A1] Divide each frequency by its class width to obtain heights 0.5, 1.6, 2.8, 3.4, 1, 2/19 . [M1] [A1] (b) The recorded hours 15 to 37 correspond to the range 14.5 to 37.5. [B1] The two partial bars contribute over widths 20.5 −14.5 = 6 and 37.5 −30.5 = 7. The two complete classes between them have frequencies 14 and 17.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q4-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The area is 6(1.6) + 14 + 17 + 7(1) = 47.6, giving about 48 [A1] electricians: [A1] (c) Cumulative frequency 32 lies in the 20.5 to 25.5 class, which starts at cumulative frequency 21. [M1] The handwritten area method uses 20 square units per electrician, so the median cuts off 32(20) = 640 square units. The first two bars contribute 10(10) + 10(32) = 420; the next bar has height 56 in this scale: Its required width is (640 −420)/56 = 3.9286 . . ., giving 20.5 + 3.9286 . . . = 24.43. Alternatively use linear interpolation: Within the median class use (m −20.5)/5 = (32 −21)/14. Interpolation gives 20.5 + 5(32 −21)/14 ≈24.43 h . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The number of hours worked in a given week by a group of 64 freelance electricians is summarized in the table below. Hours Frequency (nearest hour) 1 – 10 5 11 – 20 16 21 – 25 14 26 – 30 17 31 – 40 10 41 – 59 2 a) Draw an accurate histogram to represent this data. ( ) 5 b) Use the histogram to estimate the number of freelance electricians that worked between 15 and 37 hours during that week, correct to the nearest hour. ( ) 3 c) Estimate the median of the data. ( ) 3"
