    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-l/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A teacher is investigating the students’ method of getting back home and is told that 15% of the students gets back home by car. He decides to investigate this fact further and decides to use a random sample of 36 students across all the school’s year groups. Stating your hypotheses clearly, find the critical region … a) … for his test at the 6% level of significance. ( ) 7 b) … for a similar test where the probability of rejecting at either tail must be as iiii close as possible to 3%. ( ) 2\" style=\"--madas-width:582.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Setting hypothesis: Let p be the population proportion who go home by car and X the count in the sample: Test H0 : p = 0.15 against H1 : p̸ = 0.15. Under H0, X ∼B(36, 0.15). (a) The lower-tail probabilities are P(X ≤1) = 0.0212 [B1] and P(X ≤2) = 0.0776. [A1] The upper-tail probabilities are P(X ≥10) = 0.0351 [A1] and P(X ≥11) = 0.0137 [A1] to four decimals: [A1] Use P(X ≥10) = 1 −P(X ≤9) and P(X ≥11) = 1 −P(X ≤10) for the upper boundary: Splitting 0.06 equally gives 0.03 per tail: With a 3% limit in each tail, the critical region is X ≤1 or X ≥11 . [M1] [A1] (b) The lower-tail probability 0.0212 and upper-tail probability 0.0351 are closest to 0.03, so the region is\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"X ≤1 or X ≥10 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A teacher is investigating the students’ method of getting back home and is told that 15% of the students gets back home by car. He decides to investigate this fact further and decides to use a random sample of 36 students across all the school’s year groups. Stating your hypotheses clearly, find the critical region … a) … for his test at the 6% level of significance. ( ) 7 b) … for a similar test where the probability of rejecting at either tail must be as iiii close as possible to 3%. ( ) 2"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-l/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"In the Southgate Academy Sixth Form the students are either left handed or right handed. The following information is also known. • 0.6 of the students are female. • 0.11 of the students are left handed. • 0.10 of the female students are left handed. a) Draw a fully completed tree diagram to display the above information. ( ) 5 A student is selected at random from the Southgate Academy Sixth Form. b) Determine the probability that the student is female and right handed. ( ) 2 c) If the student is left handed, find the probability that the student is female. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"( ) d) If the student is male, find the probability that the student is left handed. 1\" style=\"--madas-width:589.5px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Drawing a tree diagram with gender first: (a) Write L, R for left- and right-handed and F, M for female and male: [B1] [A1] The four terminal branches are female right 0.6(0.9), female left 0.6(0.1), male left 0.4x, and male right 0.4(1 −x).\" style=\"--madas-width:657.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"If x = P(L | M), [A1] then 0.6(0.10) + 0.4x = 0.11, [A1] hence 0.4x = 0.11 −0.06 = 0.05, so x = 0.125 . [A1] (b) P(F ∩R) = 0.6(0.9) . [M1] [A1] = 0.54 (c) P(F | L) = 0.06/0.11 . [M1] [M1] [A1] = 6/11 (d) P(L | M) = 0.05/0.4 . [A1] = 0.125\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "In the Southgate Academy Sixth Form the students are either left handed or right handed. The following information is also known. • 0.6 of the students are female. • 0.11 of the students are left handed. • 0.10 of the female students are left handed. a) Draw a fully completed tree diagram to display the above information. ( ) 5 A student is selected at random from the Southgate Academy Sixth Form. b) Determine the probability that the student is female and right handed. ( ) 2 c) If the student is left handed, find the probability that the student is female. ( ) 3 ( ) d) If the student is male, find the probability that the student is left handed. 1"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-l/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The continuous random variable X has a Normal distribution with mean of 425 and a standard deviation of 20 . a) Determine the value of ... i. ... ( P X &gt; 455 ) ( ) 4 ii. ... ( P 395 &lt; X &lt; 455 ) ( ) 2 b) Find the value of x , given further that ( ) P 850 ( − x &lt; X &lt; x ) = 0.9722 . 5\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q7-question-2.svg\" alt=\"It is believed that the mean of X could be less than 425 , as the mean of a random sample of 12 independent observations of X was 417 . c) Test the validity of this belief, at 5% level of significance, stating clearly all the relevant quantities and hypotheses. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:589.5px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) (i) For Z = (X −425)/20, the upper endpoint standardises to 1.5, and the lower endpoint to −1.5. [M1] [A1] Hence P(X &gt; 455) = 1 −Φ(1.5) ≈0.0668 . [M1] [A1] (ii) Thus P(395 &lt; X &lt; 455) = 2Φ(1.5) −1 ≈0.8664 . [M1] [A1] (b) The total outside area is 1 −0.9722 = 0.0278, split equally into two tails of 0.0139. The bounds are symmetric about 425. [B1] [A1] The upper cumulative probability is (1 + 0.9722)/2 = 0.9861, [A1] giving z ≈2.2001 and x ≈425 + 20(2.2001) ≈469 . [M1] [A1] (c) Test H0 : µ = 425 [B1] against H1 : µ &lt; 425. [A1] Under H0, ¯X ∼N(425, 400/12). √ The observed test statistic is (417 −425)/(20/ 12) = −1.3856, [A1] giving p ≈0.0829 &gt; 0.05. [A1] Equivalently the observed −1.3856 is above the 5% lower critical value −1.6449. Fail to reject H0. [A1] The sample gives insufficient evidence that the mean is below 425. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 17 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The continuous random variable X has a Normal distribution with mean of 425 and a standard deviation of 20 . a) Determine the value of ... i. ... ( P X > 455 ) ( ) 4 ii. ... ( P 395 < X < 455 ) ( ) 2 b) Find the value of x , given further that ( ) P 850 ( − x < X < x ) = 0.9722 . 5 It is believed that the mean of X could be less than 425 , as the mean of a random sample of 12 independent observations of X was 417 . c) Test the validity of this belief, at 5% level of significance, stating clearly all the relevant quantities and hypotheses. ( ) 6"
  },
