      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-l/q12-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) If the reaction at C is R, the reaction at D is 3R. [M1] Vertical equilibrium gives R + 3R = 100g, hence R = 25g. In the moments equation divide by R to get 1.5 = 6 −3x. The centre of mass is 2 metres from A. [A1] Taking moments about it gives 1.5R = 3R(2 −x), [M1] hence x = 1.5 . [A1] (b) In the new position let the reactions be R and 2R. [M1] Taking moments about B eliminates the added weight: 3.5R + 0.75(2R) = 2(100g), R = 40g. [M1] [A1] Vertically 3R = 100g + mg, [A1] so m = 20 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
